    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Disruptions in circadian timing impair spatial and recognition memory in humans and in rodents. The complete loss of circadian timing also impairs these kinds of memory. For my project, I examined the role of circadian timing on fear memory. I used Siberian hamsters because their circadian timing can be eliminated by a simple one-time manipulation of the light-dark cycle. Arrhythmic hamsters had impaired contextual fear memory but not cued fear memory. Thus, circadian arrhythmia impaired the association of the foot shock with the context of the testing chamber, but not with the association of the tone with the chamber.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Farfan, Julia and Heller, Craig and Fernald, Russell. (2017). Role of Circadian Rhythms in Cued and Contextual Fear Conditioning . Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/vy671st6207",
